, "watch": "Once the parties settle on nominees, Colorado-specific general-election polling and any shift in the national environment — particularly a strong Republican nominee consolidating the field — could push the state toward tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Colorado has backed the Democratic presidential nominee in every recent cycle and carries a Democratic partisan lean of about six points. The 2028 contest is an open race, with the incumbent president term-limited, so neither side enjoys an incumbency advantage. No Colorado-specific general-election polls, fundraising figures, or expert ratings are available yet, so the state's partisan lean anchors the outlook.\n\nNational polling this summer offers mild Democratic comfort: surveys show leading Democrats running competitively against the top Republican contenders, and one finds a Democratic candidate ahead of a MAGA-aligned rival. With no state evidence pointing the other way, Colorado stands as a mid-single-digit Democratic edge, with enough uncertainty that nominee choices and the national environment could still reshape the race.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7597, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6966, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7137, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.701, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.713, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7202, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7103, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.7097, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.7338, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.7139, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.7218, "margin": 6